{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:099a595e6a41ad1edfda763d8cf99ff837916d026e4370a467c5e69bddf17bb7","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.1,"B":0.58,"C":0.15,"D":0.17}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"INPUTS, read off my own answers. From CQ3 (P10 0.8 / P50 1.65 / P90 2.6, sd ~0.68): P(growth >= 2.6) = 0.10; P(growth < 2.0) = 0.68; P(growth < 1.7) = 0.53. For 2032-2036 GDP-weighted OECD-38 core I must use the BACK HALF of my CQ2 path, not CQ2's 10-year average: CQ2 decomposes into 2027-2030 at ~2.85 and 2031-2036 at ~2.37, so the 2032-2036 segment central is ~2.30 (which already contains the ~+0.30pp EM-OECD floor, Turkey ~+0.27pp of it), sd ~0.45. Hence P(core_32-36 >= 2.70) = 1 - Phi((2.70-2.30)/0.45) = 1 - Phi(0.89) = 0.19; P(core_32-36 < 2.00) = Phi(-0.67) = 0.25; P(2.00-2.70) = 0.56.\nLEXICAL APPLICATION, FIRST MATCH WINS.\nStep 1, A = P(growth >= 2.6) = 0.10 -> 10.\nStep 2, C = P(core >= 2.70) x P(growth < 2.0 | core >= 2.70) = 0.19 x 0.78 (conditional lifted from the unconditional 0.68 because a high back-half core at low growth must be supply-side — the care bottleneck clearing through prices instead of administered rationing, a surviving grid wall, or outright fiscal dominance) = 0.148 -> 15.\nStep 3, D = P(core < 2.00) x P(growth < 1.7 | core < 2.00) = 0.25 x 0.66 (lifted from 0.53 for the demand-shortfall correlation: post-capex-bust excess capacity and the BIS Box C labour-income deficit produce both legs) = 0.165 -> 17.\nStep 4, B = 100 - 10 - 15 - 17 = 58. Sums to exactly 100.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED, EXPLICITLY AGAINST MY OWN CQ3 AND CQ2. (i) A = 10 is numerically identical to P(growth >= 2.6) read off my CQ3 distribution — it is not a judgement about the appeal of a productivity renaissance, and it cannot be inflated by the historical base rate (which puts ~85-90% of 1960-2019 five-year windows above 2.6% for this aggregate). My CQ3 P50 of 1.65% sits inside D's growth band, so A is upper-tail mass only. (ii) Against CQ2 = 36: CQ2's OECD conjunct marginal is 0.52 on core >= 2.50 averaged over 2027-2036, while C needs core >= 2.70 over 2032-2036 only, marginal 0.19. These are consistent ONLY because my path is front-loaded (2.85 then 2.37); on a flat path the two would conflict, and that front-loading is the single assumption a reader should check me on. Mapping 0.52 straight onto C would over-weight it by ~15pp. (iii) C + D = 32, i.e. I put under a third of the mass on the two \"something is broken\" regimes, with genuine two-sided inflation risk (0.19 up-tail vs 0.25 down-tail) rather than a one-directional stagflation lean — the honest reading of a tug-of-war whose inflationary phase is 2027-2030 and whose disinflationary phase is 2031-2036. (iv) RESIDUAL-B DECOMPOSITION, so B is an accounting residual and not a muddle-through judgement: P(1.7 <= g < 2.6) = 1 - 0.53 - 0.10 = 0.37, less the slice C takes at g in [1.7, 2.0) with core >= 2.7 (~0.038) = 0.332; plus the low-growth/moderate-core leftover at g < 1.7 with core in [2.0, 2.7) = 0.53 - 0.17 (D) - 0.11 (C's g<1.7 share) = 0.25. Total 0.332 + 0.25 = 0.582, matching the residual 0.58 to within rounding. So only ~33 of my 58 is the literal barbell; ~25 is the leftover cell the lock assigns to B, which a naive reader would have put in D. (v) D at 17 is held below the ~25 that my growth distribution alone would suggest because D's second condition, core < 2.00 on a GDP-weighted basket containing Turkey, Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Costa Rica, requires G7 core at ~1.65-1.70 — a deeper Japanification than the narrative \"D\" pictures. That is an index-construction constraint, not a macro view, and it moves ~6pp from D to B.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.756Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkish annual CPI, as published by TurkStat, averages below 7.0% over calendar 2027-2029 (three-year arithmetic mean of the December-on-December prints), with Mexico, Chile and Colombia each averaging below 3.5% over the same three years.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the EM-OECD floor that my CQ2 depends on: the GDP-weighted OECD-38 core aggregate falls from 2.556 to ~2.40, and P(core >= 2.50 over 2027-2036) drops from 0.52 to ~0.33. CQ2 moves from 36 to ~24. It also lowers the back-half core central from 2.30 to ~2.15, cutting CQ6-C from 15 to ~10 and raising D from 17 to ~22, with B roughly unchanged. This is the cleanest single falsifier of my numbers because it attacks the term I have made the largest and most mechanical.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US CPI shelter (BLS series for shelter, December-on-December) averages below 2.4%/yr over calendar 2027-2030, while US medical care services plus the social-assistance component of PCE services averages below 2.6%/yr over the same four years.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Breaks the two line items that carry 1.35pp of my 2.335 US core build. US core falls to ~1.95, the OECD-38 aggregate to ~2.40, and the front-loaded path I rely on (2.85 then 2.37) collapses to a flat ~2.2. CQ2 moves from 36 to ~22; CQ6-C from 15 to ~9 and D from 17 to ~24. It would also confirm the state-monopsony-rations-care channel over the care-prices-clear channel, which lowers CQ1's second conjunct further (from 0.12 to ~0.07) and so CQ1 from 8 to ~5.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Announced hyperscaler plus utility-scale firm-capacity additions serving data centres in the OECD-38 and China reach cumulative in-service (commercial-operation-date) capacity of at least 70 GW of net firm supply by end-2031, as reported in